window.SEARCH_INDEX=[{"t":"Statistics starts with a question","u":"module-1.html#intro","g":"Module 1 notes","x":"Statistics is about gathering, organizing, analyzing, and interpreting data. Before any of that, answer three questions: what group or scientific question are we focusing on, which members of that group (or which questions about it) do we want to explore, and how will we gather and organize the data? Identify the population, the sample, and the variable in a study. Classify a variable as categorical (nominal or ordinal) or quantitative (discrete or continuous). Recognize simple random, systematic, stratified, and cluster sampling. Describe a distribution by its shape, center, spread, and outliers. Compute the mean, median, quartiles, IQR, sample variance, and sample standard deviation by hand, and identify outliers. Predict what shifting or rescaling data does to x̄ and s, and choose summaries that fit the data. Example scientific question Three-toed sloths Is it true that three-toed sloths move at an average speed of 4 meters per minute? We cannot time every sloth, so we time a sample of sloths and use their speeds to learn about all of them. This question returns in Chapters 7 and 8."},{"t":"Population, sample, and variables","u":"module-1.html#population","g":"Module 1 notes","x":"The population is the complete collection of all measurements or data being considered. It is not necessarily people, it often has millions or billions (even infinitely many) members, and it is generally never fully observed. A sample is a subset of the population, usually a small percentage of it. A variable is any characteristic of an individual that is of interest; it changes from subject to subject. There are two types. Categorical (qualitative): each observation belongs to one of several groups. Nominal groups have no order (blood type A, B, AB, O; eye color). Ordinal groups have a natural order (socioeconomic status low, medium, high; education level). Quantitative: observations are numbers for which arithmetic makes sense. Discrete variables take only certain fixed values with nothing in between (number of computers owned, number of pets, age in whole years). Continuous variables can take infinitely many values between any two points (height, weight, income). Notation. Lowercase letters name variables: x might be the weight of newborn calves, y the number of defective items in a shipment, z a patient's blood type. A sample of size n from x is written x1, x2, , xn . Worked example Identify the population, sample, and variable (a) A survey of 1900 students from college campuses across the US found an average student loan debt of $42,315. (b) A 2018 Brookings poll wanted the percentage of adult US internet users who believe robots will perform most human activities in 30 years. (c) A 2019 ESPN poll found that 68% of adult NBA fans believe the Mavericks have the best future among non-playoff teams. (a) Population: all US college students. Sample: the 1900 students surveyed. Variable: student loan debt in dollars. Type: quantitative and continuous. (Money is often treated as continuous because it takes so many values.) (b) Population: all adult internet users in the US. Sample: the adults polled by Brookings. Variable: whether the person believes robots will perform most human activities in 30 years (yes or no). Type: categorical and nominal. (c) Population: all adult NBA fans. Sample: the fans in the ESPN poll. Variable: whether the fan believes the Mavericks have the best future among non-playoff teams (yes or no). Type: categorical and nominal. The 68% summarizes the sample, not the whole population. Watch out. Numbers are not always quantitative. A zip code or a jersey number is a label, so it is categorical. A percentage reported for a yes/no question (like the 68%) summarizes a categorical variable."},{"t":"Sampling methods","u":"module-1.html#sampling","g":"Module 1 notes","x":"How the sample is chosen decides whether it can stand in for the population. Four methods appear in this course. Simple random sampling (SRS): every possible sample of a given size has an equal chance of being selected. Systematic sampling: pick a random starting point between 1 and k, then include every kth subject after it. Stratified sampling: divide the population into homogeneous groups called strata (members are similar with respect to the variable of interest), then take an SRS within each stratum. Cluster sampling: divide the population into heterogeneous groups called clusters (each cluster looks like the population as a whole), randomly select clusters, and include everyone in the chosen clusters. Worked example An SRS of size 2 from {A, B, C, D} List every possible sample of size 2: {A,B}, {A,C}, {A,D}, {B,C}, {B,D}, {C,D}. There are 6. In an SRS each of the 6 samples is equally likely, so each has probability 1/6. Each individual appears in 3 of the 6 samples, so each individual is chosen with probability 3/6 = 1/2. Equal chances for individuals alone is not enough for an SRS; every sample must be equally likely. Worked example Systematic sampling A quality engineer wants a sample from 500 items coming off a line and chooses k = 25. Choose a random start between 1 and 25, say 7. Take every 25th item after it: 7, 32, 57, 82, … The jth item chosen is in position 7 + (j − 1)(25). The sample has 500/25 = 20 items. The 20th is in position 7 + 19(25) = 482. Stratified or cluster? In stratified sampling you sample within every group, and the groups are internally similar. In cluster sampling you sample whole groups , and each group is a small copy of the population."},{"t":"Distributions: tables and graphs","u":"module-1.html#distribution","g":"Module 1 notes","x":"The distribution of a variable describes how often (frequency) or what fraction of the time (relative frequency) the variable takes each value. Some outcomes are more likely than others: adult heights fall between 5 and 6 feet far more often than between 3 and 4 feet. The population distribution is usually unknown, so we study the distribution of a sample and look for its shape, center, spread, and outliers . relative frequency= frequencyn Here n is the total number of observations. Frequency tables give the count (and relative frequency, as a proportion or percentage) for each value or range of values. Pie charts and bar charts summarize categorical variables. Histograms are bar graphs of binned quantitative data; the height of each bar is the frequency (or relative frequency) of values in that bin. Boxplots graph the five-number summary (Section 6). Reading a histogram. Where are the values concentrated? How spread out are they? Is the shape symmetric or skewed? Are there gaps or clusters? Are there potential outliers? A histogram of hits per nine-inning game rises smoothly to a single peak and then declines, extending a bit further to the right than to the left: a slight positive (right) skew . Skew is named for the longer tail. Worked example Salary survey: are both majors equally represented? A university surveyed 2,232 recent alumni who majored in engineering or education. The variables are gender (categorical, nominal), major (categorical, nominal), and salary in dollars (quantitative, continuous). There were 1,076 education majors and 1,156 engineering majors. Total: 1,076 + 1,156 = 2,232. Relative frequency of education: 1,076/2,232 = 0.4821, about 48.2%. Relative frequency of engineering: 1,156/2,232 = 0.5179, about 51.8%. The relative frequencies add to 1. The two majors are close to equally represented, with slightly more engineers. R counts <- c(Education = 1076, Engineering = 1156); counts / sum(counts) Python counts = {\"Education\": 1076, \"Engineering\": 1156}; n = sum(counts.values()); {k: v / n for k, v in counts.items()}"},{"t":"Measures of location: mean, median, mode","u":"module-1.html#location","g":"Module 1 notes","x":"The sample mean uses every observation. The sample median x is the middle observation once the values are sorted. The sample mode is the value (or values) that occurs most often. x= x1+x2+ +xnn= 1n i=1nxi n odd: x = the ( n+12 )th ordered value n even: x = average of the ( n2 )th and ( n2+1 )th values Worked example Sloth speeds Seven sloths were timed (meters per minute): 4.3, 2.8, 4.0, 5.1, 3.4, 4.6, 3.7. Sum: 4.3 + 2.8 + 4.0 + 5.1 + 3.4 + 4.6 + 3.7 = 27.9. Mean: x̄ = 27.9/7 = 3.9857 m/min . Sort: 2.8, 3.4, 3.7, 4.0, 4.3, 4.6, 5.1. n = 7 is odd, so the median is the (7 + 1)/2 = 4th ordered value: x̃ = 4.0 m/min . No value repeats, so there is no single mode. Now suppose the 5.1 had been recorded as 10.8. The sum becomes 33.6 and x̄ = 4.8 m/min , but the 4th ordered value is still 4.0. The median is resistant to the extreme value; the mean is pulled toward it. R x <- c(4.3, 2.8, 4.0, 5.1, 3.4, 4.6, 3.7); mean(x); median(x) Python import numpy as np; x = np.array([4.3, 2.8, 4.0, 5.1, 3.4, 4.6, 3.7]); x.mean(), np.median(x) Common mistakes. Forgetting to sort before finding the median. With an even n, picking one of the two middle values instead of averaging them. Reporting the position (n + 1)/2 instead of the value in that position."},{"t":"Quartiles, percentiles, and boxplots","u":"module-1.html#quartiles","g":"Module 1 notes","x":"The pth percentile is a value with about p × 100% of the data below it and about (1 − p) × 100% above it. The first quartile Q1 is the 25th percentile, the second quartile Q2 is the 50th percentile (the median), and the third quartile Q3 is the 75th percentile. The five-number summary is min, Q1 , Q2 , Q3 , max. Our convention for quartiles. Sort the data and find the median. Split the data into a lower half and an upper half, leaving the median out of both halves when n is odd . Q1 is the median of the lower half and Q3 is the median of the upper half. Five-number summary: ,\\;Q1,\\; x,\\;Q3,\\; IQR=Q3-Q1 Worked example Five-number summary by hand Ten observations: 1, 2, 7, 11, 12, 18, 23, 25, 98, 197. The data are already sorted. n = 10 is even, so the median is (12 + 18)/2 = 15. Lower half: 1, 2, 7, 11, 12, so Q1 = 7 . Upper half: 18, 23, 25, 98, 197, so Q3 = 25 . Five-number summary: (1, 7, 15, 25, 197). IQR = 25 − 7 = 18. R x <- c(1, 2, 7, 11, 12, 18, 23, 25, 98, 197); median(x[1:5]); median(x[6:10]) R's quantile(x) and summary(x) interpolate and may differ from our convention. Python import numpy as np; x = np.sort([1, 2, 7, 11, 12, 18, 23, 25, 98, 197]); np.median(x[:5]), np.median(x[5:]) np.percentile interpolates and may differ from our convention. Boxplots and histograms show the same features: shape, center, spread, and outliers. The box runs from Q1 to Q3 with a line at the median; whiskers reach to the most extreme values that are not outliers, and outliers are plotted separately. Skewness in a histogram appears in the boxplot as a longer whisker (longer tail) on the side of the skew . Watch out. With an odd n, do not include the median in either half. And do not confuse the IQR ( Q3 - Q1 ) with the range (max − min)."},{"t":"Measures of variability","u":"module-1.html#variability","g":"Module 1 notes","x":"The deviations from the mean are x1- x,\\;x2- x, ,xn- x . They always add to zero, so their plain average cannot measure spread; we square them first. i=1n(xi- x)=0 The interquartile range , IQR=Q3-Q1 , is the range of the middle 50% of the data. The textbook calls it the fourth spread , fs . The sample variance averages the squared deviations with the divisor n − 1, and the sample standard deviation is its square root, back in the original units. s2= 1n-1 i=1n(xi- x)2= Sxxn-1 Sxx= i=1nxi2- 1n ( i=1nxi )2 s= s2 The divisor n − 1 accounts for the one degree of freedom lost by estimating the center with x̄. Both s2 and s are always nonnegative, and s = 0 only when every observation is identical. Worked example s two ways for 2, 4, 5, 9, 10 Mean: x̄ = (2 + 4 + 5 + 9 + 10)/5 = 30/5 = 6. Deviations: −4, −2, −1, 3, 4. They add to 0. Squared deviations: 16, 4, 1, 9, 16. Sum: Sxx=46 . Shortcut check: xi2=4+16+25+81+100=226 , and Sxx=226- 3025=226-180=46 . Same answer. Variance: s2=46/(5-1)=11.5 . Standard deviation: s= 11.5=3.3912 . R x <- c(2, 4, 5, 9, 10); var(x); sd(x) (R divides by n − 1) Python import numpy as np; x = np.array([2, 4, 5, 9, 10]); x.var(ddof=1), x.std(ddof=1) ( ddof=1 gives n − 1; the default divides by n) Red-pencil reminder. Divide by n − 1, not n. Do not report the variance when the standard deviation is asked for. In the shortcut, square the sum after adding: ( xi)2 is not xi2 ."},{"t":"Outliers","u":"module-1.html#outliers","g":"Module 1 notes","x":"Outliers are values far outside the overall pattern of a distribution. Always try to explain an outlier; it may be a recording error, or it may be the most interesting point in the data. A common rule of thumb flags an observation as an outlier when it lies beyond 1.5 IQR from the box. x<Q1-1.5 IQR or x>Q3+1.5 IQR Boxplots often separate mild outliers (beyond 1.5 IQR) from extreme outliers (beyond 3 IQR), as in the nitrogen-load boxplot from class. Worked example Salary fences by major Summary statistics for salary: Education Q1=34,478.5 , Q3=36,519.5 ; Engineering Q1=54,092 , Q3=57,799 . Education: IQR = 36,519.5 − 34,478.5 = 2,041. Fences: 34,478.5 − 1.5(2,041) = 31,417 and 36,519.5 + 1.5(2,041) = 39,581. Engineering: IQR = 57,799 − 54,092 = 3,707. Fences: 54,092 − 1.5(3,707) = 48,531.5 and 57,799 + 1.5(3,707) = 63,359.5. An education salary of $40,000 would be an outlier for education majors (above 39,581), while the same salary is far below every engineering fence: an outlier there in the other direction. \"Outlier\" always means \"unusual relative to this distribution.\" About outliers. Never delete an outlier just because it is extreme. Fix it if it is a recording error; otherwise keep it. If you report results without it, say so and say why."},{"t":"Shifting and scaling data","u":"module-1.html#transform","g":"Module 1 notes","x":"Changing units changes the summaries in a predictable way. Adding a constant moves every value, and the center, by that constant but leaves the spread alone. Multiplying by a positive constant multiplies both the center and the spread. yj=xj+C\\; \\; y= x+C,\\;\\;sy=sx yj=Cxj\\;(C>0)\\; \\; y=C x,\\;\\;sy=Csx Proof sketch Why the proposition holds Shift: y= 1n (xj+C)= x+C . Each deviation is unchanged: yj- y=(xj+C)-( x+C)=xj- x , so sy=sx . Scale: y= 1n Cxj=C x . Each deviation is multiplied by C: yj- y=C(xj- x) , so sy2=C2sx2 and sy=Csx because C > 0. Worked example Sloth speeds in other units For the seven sloths, x̄ = 3.9857 m/min and s = 0.7690 m/min . In centimeters per minute every speed is multiplied by 100: ȳ = 398.57 cm/min and sy = 76.90 cm/min . If the stopwatch added 0.2 m/min to every reading, the mean becomes 4.1857 m/min and s stays 0.7690 m/min . Watch out. A shift never changes s (or the IQR). A scale factor multiplies s by C, but multiplies the variance by C2 ."},{"t":"Choosing measures of location and variability","u":"module-1.html#choosing","g":"Module 1 notes","x":"For symmetric data without outliers , the sample mean x̄ and the sample standard deviation s are good summaries. For skewed data or data with outliers , the median x̃ and the IQR are usually preferred, because they are resistant. In skewed data the mean is pulled toward the longer tail. Worked example Do salaries differ across majors? Summary statistics: Education n = 1,076, mean $35,183.06, median $35,640.50; Engineering n = 1,156, mean $56,004.89, median $55,660. Center: the engineering median is about $20,000 higher than the education median. Salaries clearly differ by major. Spread: the engineering IQR (3,707) is larger than the education IQR (2,041). Shape: for education the mean is below the median, which suggests a slight left skew; for engineering the mean is above the median, which suggests a slight right skew. Because the shapes are not perfectly symmetric, comparing medians and IQRs is the safer choice. Describe a distribution in context. Name the shape, give a center and a spread that suit the shape, mention outliers, and include units."},{"t":"Summary","u":"module-1.html#summary","g":"Module 1 notes","x":"Identify the population, sample, and variable, and classify the variable. Choose a display that fits the variable: bar or pie chart for categorical data, histogram or boxplot for quantitative data. Describe shape, center, spread, and outliers. x= 1n xi s2= Sxxn-1 IQR=Q3-Q1 outlier: beyond Q1-1.5\\,IQR or Q3+1.5\\,IQR R and Python for engineers. Work by hand first. To check: R uses mean , median , var , sd , IQR , boxplot , hist ; Python (NumPy) uses np.mean , np.median , np.var(x, ddof=1) , np.std(x, ddof=1) , and Matplotlib's plt.hist and plt.boxplot . Software quartiles (and so the IQR) can differ slightly from our hand convention."},{"t":"standard normal table","u":"tables.html#formulas","g":"Tables & formulas","x":"The standard normal table The entries give left area P(Z<z) , rounded to four decimals. Round z to two decimals. Negative rows use a negative hundredths digit too: row −1.6, column .04 means −1.64. Look up z → left area Left area → z Value Find in table For z beyond ±3.49, the course treats left area as essentially 0 or 1. For inverse lookups, choose the closest four-decimal entry. Ties use the midpoint z, such as .9500 → 1.645."},{"t":"formula sheet","u":"tables.html","g":"Tables & formulas","x":"The formula sheet The formulas follow our course conventions. Work each one by hand first. Center x= 1n i=1n xi x=middle ordered value Spread s2= Sxxn-1 Sxx= (xi- x)2= xi2- ( xi)2n s= s2 Quartiles and outliers IQR=Q3-Q1 outlier if x<Q1-1.5\\,IQR or x>Q3+1.5\\,IQR Shifting and scaling y=x+C:\\ y= x+C,\\ sy=sx y=Cx:\\ y=C x,\\ sy=Csx Formulas for each chapter are added here as its module is completed. The t and χ² tables arrive with Modules 7 and 8."}];
